Fuzz targets for the VFS495 driver
==================================

The driver runs inside fprintd, as root, and parses bytes it cannot trust: whatever arrives
from the USB device (which could be something pretending to be the reader) and the command
file. These targets feed those parsers arbitrary input:

  fuzz-commands   the command file parser                       (vfs495-commands.c)
  fuzz-records    record layer, handshake reply, ServerHello,
                  signed patches against a flash block          (vfs495-protocol.c)
  fuzz-frames     the swipe frame parser                        (vfs495-image.c)
  fuzz-assemble   the stitcher and swipe verdict                (vfs495-assemble.c)

Seeds come from private captures (make-corpus.py writes captures/fuzz/TARGET). Every input a
fuzzer finds that crashed something belongs in that corpus after the fix, so the regression
run keeps testing it.

Two builds:

  Replay (any compiler): each target is linked with run-corpus.c and `meson test` replays the
  corpus. In the sanitizer build this is the regression check:
    driver/tools/hardening-check.sh

  Fuzzing (clang, libFuzzer):
    CC=clang meson setup build/fuzz driver -Dfuzz=true -Db_sanitize=address,undefined -Db_lundef=false
    CC=clang meson setup build/fuzz-asan driver -Dfuzz=true -Db_sanitize=address -Db_lundef=false
    ninja -C build/fuzz && ninja -C build/fuzz-asan
    driver/fuzz/run-fuzzers.sh [SECONDS]     (default 600 per target, all four in parallel)

  fuzz-assemble runs from the ASan-only build: under libFuzzer with UBSan the stitcher is about
  10x slower (2.8 s against 0.26 s for 150 lines; both sanitizers without libFuzzer: 0.1 s).
  Its corpus is replayed under ASan and UBSan by the regression check.
